WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.290
Priya signs the group health contract for her bakery.

00:00:03.290 --> 00:00:08.042
That document is the master policy, and Priya, the employer, is the policyholder.

00:00:08.042 --> 00:00:10.966
Her employees are not parties to the contract.

00:00:10.966 --> 00:00:14.622
Each covered employee receives a certificate of coverage, sometimes called

00:00:14.622 --> 00:00:18.277
a certificate of insurance or evidence of coverage, describing the

00:00:18.277 --> 00:00:21.933
benefits, exclusions, cost sharing and how to file a claim.

00:00:21.933 --> 00:00:26.319
The certificate is a summary; the master policy controls if they differ.

00:00:26.319 --> 00:00:28.878
Dependents are covered through the employee’s certificate.

00:00:28.878 --> 00:00:32.899
When a question asks who the insured is in group coverage,

00:00:32.899 --> 00:00:36.920
the answer is the employee; who owns the policy, the employer.

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:40.897
Because the certificate is all most employees ever see, Maryland

00:00:40.897 --> 00:00:44.874
and federal law require it to be accurate and complete.

00:00:44.874 --> 00:00:49.249
It must describe the benefits and limitations, the conditions for eligibility,

00:00:49.249 --> 00:00:53.624
how coverage ends and the continuation and conversion rights that follow.

00:00:53.624 --> 00:00:58.396
Maryland’s continuation statutes specifically require each certificate to explain how a surviving

00:00:58.396 --> 00:01:03.169
spouse, a divorced spouse or a terminated employee may elect continued coverage.

00:01:03.169 --> 00:01:06.350
The Affordable Care Act added the summary of

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:09.532
benefits and coverage, a standardized four-page comparison document.

00:01:09.532 --> 00:01:15.100
A certificate that misstates benefits exposes the insurer to claims for the benefits described.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:17.988
How is the group’s premium set?

00:01:17.988 --> 00:01:24.726
Experience rating bases next year’s premium on this group’s own claims history, adjusted for

00:01:24.726 --> 00:01:31.465
trend and expenses; it is used for large groups whose claims are statistically credible.

00:01:31.465 --> 00:01:38.203
Community rating charges all groups in an area the same rate regardless of claims.

00:01:38.203 --> 00:01:44.460
Federal and Maryland law now require modified community rating for small employers: premiums

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:50.717
may vary only by age within a 3 to 1 band, tobacco use,

00:01:50.717 --> 00:01:56.974
geographic rating area and family composition, never by the group’s health or claims.

00:01:56.974 --> 00:02:02.750
Priya’s twenty-person bakery is community rated; a 2,000-employee hospital is experience rated.

00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:07.813
Group insurance works because the group formed for some purpose other than buying insurance.

00:02:07.813 --> 00:02:12.153
Employees come together to work, union members to bargain, borrowers to borrow.

00:02:12.153 --> 00:02:15.408
That natural purpose means the group’s health is roughly

00:02:15.408 --> 00:02:18.663
average and no one joined because they were sick.

00:02:18.663 --> 00:02:21.918
A group assembled solely to buy insurance would attract

00:02:21.918 --> 00:02:25.173
the people who most need it, defeating the pricing.

00:02:25.173 --> 00:02:29.513
State laws therefore define the eligible groups, require a minimum size in

00:02:29.513 --> 00:02:33.853
some cases, and in Maryland permit true small employers down to a

00:02:33.853 --> 00:02:37.470
single eligible employee through the small group market and SHOP.

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:42.376
Employer groups cover the employees of a single employer, the most common form.

00:02:42.376 --> 00:02:45.772
Creditor groups cover borrowers; the lender is policyholder and

00:02:45.772 --> 00:02:49.168
the coverage pays the debt, as in credit disability.

00:02:49.168 --> 00:02:54.074
Labor unions cover members, often through trusts the union and employers fund jointly.

00:02:54.074 --> 00:02:58.224
Multiple employer trusts pool small employers, usually in the same industry,

00:02:58.224 --> 00:03:02.375
so they can buy coverage on better terms; Maryland regulates these

00:03:02.375 --> 00:03:05.771
multiple employer welfare arrangements closely because some have failed.

00:03:05.771 --> 00:03:09.922
Associations formed for purposes other than insurance, typically professional or trade

00:03:09.922 --> 00:03:14.073
associations in existence for at least two years, may cover members.

00:03:14.073 --> 00:03:15.960
Credit unions cover their members.

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:19.401
When a question names an organization, ask two things.

00:03:19.401 --> 00:03:22.842
Was it formed for a purpose other than insurance?

00:03:22.842 --> 00:03:28.195
A trade association that has existed for years and happens to offer a health

00:03:28.195 --> 00:03:33.165
plan qualifies; a club organized last month to get cheap insurance does not.

00:03:33.165 --> 00:03:36.606
Who holds the master policy and who receives certificates?

00:03:36.606 --> 00:03:41.576
In an employer group it is the employer and employees; in a creditor

00:03:41.576 --> 00:03:46.164
group, the lender and borrowers; in an association, the association and members.

00:03:46.164 --> 00:03:50.752
Then ask how the premium is set: by the group’s own experience

00:03:50.752 --> 00:03:55.340
if large, by modified community rating if it is a small employer.
